vered  ways to use haptic technology and  ultrasound to create 3d shapes by  focusing complex patterns of ultrasound  onto our hands it causes air  disturbances which we can then see and  even feel head researcher dr. Ben Long  believes the tech could be used by  doctors to explore CT scans using the  haptic feedback to field diseases such  as tumors that's incredible enough by  itself but imagine if the technology was  used in conjunction with VR headsets  if you've read ready player one by  Ernest Cline and this is basically the  Oasis simulator in real life  one great potential market for VR  headsets is that of horror games games  like outlast amnesia and alien isolation  are already playable in the rift and  according to YouTube videos well it  looks like a pretty terrifying  experience I can't decide if it's the  actual jump scare itself that's the  worst or the build up the constant  lingering feel of dread because you  think something's there  interestingly researchers are now saying  that they've uncovered just what causes  that feeling of a presence phenomenon a  team led by Olaf Blanca in Switzerland  worked with a range of epilepsy migraine  and stroke patients and discovered that  they were perceiving presences due to  the damage in three particular areas of  their brains these areas are responsible  for integrating sensory  signals from both inside and outside the  body but due to damage they were  confused causing the phenomenon so  that's the why but the researchers also  figured out how to induce this  phenomenon in people without brain  damage by building a robot of course in  front of the volunteers was a master  robot arm and by moving it the  volunteers got a bit of touch feedback  in their fingers this also caused  another robot behind them this slave  robot to copy the motion simultaneously  by drawing on the volunteers backs  remove the touch feedback and delay the  slave robot by just 500 milliseconds and  the mismatch in brain signals induc